SECTIONS=[]
def section(id,title,body): SECTIONS.append((id,title,body))

section('overview','1. Project overview', '''
<p><strong>EquipRent is an equipment-rental marketplace, initially oriented to Hyderabad.</strong> The repository contains independent Expo customer and partner apps; the partner binary serves owners and operators. Marketing and the platform-operations console are separate repositories. This is an API migration/design problem: there is no standalone Node.js API implementation in the supplied project.</p>
<p><strong>Observed stack:</strong> TypeScript, React Native, Expo Router, pnpm workspaces, Supabase JS, PostgreSQL 17 configuration, Supabase Auth/Storage/Realtime, SQL functions/triggers/RLS, pg_cron and some pg_net scaffolding. The current package manifest specifies Expo ~57.0.27, React 19.2.3 and React Native 0.86.3; older README version tables are stale. Jest, Node tests, Playwright tooling and isolated SQL suites already exist.</p>
<p>The client path is screen → hook/context → service → direct PostgREST/RPC, Auth, Storage or Edge Function. Services expose snake_case row contracts and contexts often map them to camelCase. Existing services have mixed return/error conventions and recovery reads after uncertain writes; a migration adapter must preserve those semantics until clients change.</p>
<p><strong>Local inventory:</strong> 243 SQL migration files and five Edge Function entry points: <code>razorpay-payment</code>, <code>razorpay-webhook</code>, <code>operator-access-payment</code>, <code>mobilization-quote</code>, and <code>customer-account-session</code>. The supplied audit snapshot reports 114 application tables and 591 functions; those are snapshot claims, not a fresh hosted measurement. A separate untracked normalized proposal describes 120 tables across 16 business schemas. It is not the current runtime schema.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Actor</th><th>Existing responsibilities and identity implications</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Guest</td><td>Sanitized machine catalogue and featured discovery. Guest projections must never expose owner/operator phone numbers, documents or booking identities.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Individual/company customer</td><td>Browse, save sites, rent machines with/without an operator, request operators for own machines, pay, participate in custody, raise support/disputes, review and request account deletion. The same phone can select distinct account UUIDs; company mode is not evidence of a multi-seat organization system.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Owner</td><td>Fleet and attachment inventory, verification evidence, publication, availability blocks, request acceptance, roster/assignment management, self-operation, return inspections, wages and earnings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator</td><td>Reviewed identity/licence/skills, availability/duty, roster consent, job offers, custody and location, overtime and wage acknowledgement. The legacy profile role enum is customer/owner/admin; operator identity and admission involve additional records. Do not implement authorization as a single role enum.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staff/admin</td><td>Capability- and scope-controlled verification, dispatch, support, moderation and finance operations are present in SQL. The complete admin UI and all its contracts are outside this folder.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Primary journey:</strong> discover → validate fit/availability → checkout hold → request → owner acceptance and staffing → verified advance/full payment → dispatch/pickup → arrival/handover → work → overtime/balance clearance → release/completion → yard return and inspection → settlement/reviews. Machine-only pickup and owner-self operation have separate permitted actors. Cancellation can leave a physical-return obligation and refund liability.</p>
<p><strong>Keep independent tracks:</strong> booking lifecycle (<code>Requested → Confirmed → Dispatched → On Site → In Use → Completed</code>, plus cancellation), assignment, physical custody, payment, overtime, transport, damage and settlement. Completion alone must not free a machine that has not returned or authorize a payout under hold.</p>
<p><strong>Current rule details that must survive migration:</strong> checkout holds last five minutes; a Requested booking remains provisional and confirmation is the durable reservation boundary. Existing cross-customer scheduling applies a two-hour turnaround rule. The latest cancellation migration grants the customer a free window strictly before two minutes after the first verified booking collection; later balance/overtime/damage payments do not reset that timestamp. Cancellation can set the legacy payment status to <code>Refunded</code> while <code>refund_state='owed'</code>: expose the liability and actual disbursement separately. Optional insurance/protection is rejected by current pricing SQL, and new customer cash-overtime reporting is rejected; legacy cash reconciliation paths remain.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Integration</th><th>Evidence and status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Supabase</td><td>Current identity, PostgreSQL API/RLS, private/public media and realtime. Provider dependencies extend into SQL roles, auth.uid(), auth.users FKs, storage policies and scheduled jobs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Razorpay</td><td>Incoming rental/damage/access order and capture verification code exists; webhook handles supported captures/failures and operator-access refund revocation. No automatic outbound payout/refund executor is established by this review.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phone SMS</td><td>Supabase phone OTP; Twilio configuration is discussed, but actual hosted provider readiness is unverified. Local config contains fixed test OTP entries; never carry those into production.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maps/routing</td><td>Google Routes for server-side mobilization road-distance quotes; native Google Maps rendering; Nominatim geocoding documented. Straight-line discovery distance must not become billable road mileage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Durable in-app inbox and realtime exist. Device-token/push scaffolding is not proof of working background OS push.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proposals/placeholders</td><td>AWS replacement and normalized database redesign are proposals. AI document extraction/support was removed from this app; issuer integrations (DigiLocker/Vahan/Sarathi) and urgent IVR are not established implementations. Do not add them to MVP scope.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
''')

section('requirements','2. API requirements and unresolved decisions', '''
<table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Functional requirement derived from current code</th><th>Acceptance condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>F01</td><td>Phone proof, app admission, separate customer accounts, exclusive partner role and device-session binding</td><td>Wrong app/account selection cannot inherit another account's bookings or invalidate an unrelated session before successful admission. Refresh, logout, replacement and deleted-account checks work.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F02</td><td>Fleet/catalogue, guest-safe search, stock, compatibility, publication and dated availability</td><td>Server filters and limits results; inactive or unverified inventory is not bookable. Fit and quantity are independently checked inside booking creation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F03</td><td>Atomic request creation, checkout holds, recovery, price snapshots and valid transitions</td><td>Concurrent customers cannot acquire the same resource window. Same request key returns the same booking; different payload with that key conflicts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F04</td><td>Staffing, roster invitations, operator eligibility, owner-self operation and operator-only hires</td><td>Licence validity spans the job, reviewed skills match, accepted assignments do not overlap, and wage/access terms are frozen before acceptance.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F05</td><td>Four custody challenges, condition/media evidence, attachment quantities and cancellation return</td><td>Only designated holder sees a code; only its counterparty verifies it; replay cannot advance twice. Resource release respects physical return and all attachment obligations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F06</td><td>Payments, advances/balances, transport, overtime, deposits, payouts and wage receipt acknowledgement</td><td>Amounts and currency come from server obligations. Only verified captured money updates collection. A ledger liability or reported cash payment is not automatically a transfer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F07</td><td>Support, incidents, two damage-claim tracks and disputes</td><td>Actor/time-window checks, evidence visibility and financial holds are preserved. Customer damage recovery remains distinct from owner/operator claims.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F08</td><td>Reviews, favorites, demand offers, gated referral attribution, inbox and account closure</td><td>Review targets derive from participation; public comments follow consent/moderation; no invented referral reward balance; deletion checks live obligations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F09</td><td>Background expiry, matching, settlement eligibility, document expiry, support SLA and account purge</td><td>Behaviors continue with no app open; retries are bounded/idempotent; only one scheduler owns each job during cutover.</td></tr>
<tr><td>F10</td><td>Reports and operational control</td><td>Scoped owner/operator earnings/utilisation and staff reconciliation use stated metric definitions and preserve finance/dispatch capability restrictions.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Non-functional requirements:</strong> strict DTO validation; object/field authorization; transaction integrity; recoverable retries on mobile networks; bounded search, report windows and payloads; private evidence; auditable money and privileged decisions; structured redacted logs; compatible client upgrades; deterministic migration/restore; event reconnect/resync and graceful shutdown. These follow from existing workflows even where the current implementation is incomplete.</p>
<p><strong>Proposed targets requiring confirmation:</strong> 99.9% API availability, p95 ≤300 ms for ordinary cached/indexed reads and ≤800 ms for internal transactional commands, excluding external provider latency and large reports; provider calls get explicit bounded timeouts and recoverable pending states. Proposed recovery objectives: RPO ≤15 minutes and RTO ≤4 hours, subject to backup/deployment budget. Validate with measured peak search, checkout, GPS and realtime loads; the planning document's 10,000 MAU and older one-million-user scenarios are not measured concurrency requirements.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Finding or open decision</th><th>Required resolution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Blocker</td><td>API facade versus complete Supabase replacement</td><td>Choose identity/storage/realtime/scheduler ownership. Lowest-risk recommendation is a staged Node API over the existing database/auth first. If the AWS migration decision is already approved, implement provider-neutral identity and port database dependencies before cutover.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blocker</td><td>Schema source of truth and drift</td><td>The supplied 18 October audit reports missing custody RPCs/tables/columns despite a recorded migration, plus hosted-only migration 20261015122558. Reconcile live definitions, triggers, privileges and required capabilities against local SQL. Do not infer deployment from migration filenames/history.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blocker</td><td>Current schema versus normalized proposal</td><td>Approve an ADR. Retain current schema for initial parity unless a funded redesign/backfill is selected. The proposal adds accounts/memberships, financial journals and other structures that are not current product requirements.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blocker</td><td>Actual data preservation and external consumers</td><td>Planning files call development data disposable; obtain current confirmation before any destructive migration. Inventory admin/marketing callers, jobs, storage objects and webhooks; review the separate console repository before declaring full platform parity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>High</td><td>Payment and operating policy</td><td>Confirm cancellation windows and policy versions, deposit settlement, retention release, manual versus automated refunds/payouts, operator-only-hire payment handling, operator access mode, and transport/mobilization distinctions. The latest cancellation SQL supersedes older prose; port it exactly before changing policy.</td></tr>
<tr><td>High</td><td>Production identity and providers</td><td>Verify SMS delivery/provider setup, remove fixed OTP bypasses from production, enforce disabled placeholder payments, verify Razorpay test/live separation and route API credentials. Review account-switch/session minting specifically.</td></tr>
<tr><td>High</td><td>Legal/commercial configuration</td><td>Stakeholders and qualified reviewers must approve supplier/tax invoice identity, insurance offering, data/document retention, location purpose, grievance contacts and financial evidence policy. Repository legal drafts are not verified production compliance.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medium</td><td>Scale, geography and reporting</td><td>Confirm launch territory, actual load/SLOs, working-day/timezone rules and definitions of earned/paid/held/utilised. UAE and generalized multi-currency behavior remain future scope.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medium</td><td>Client compatibility</td><td>Choose supported app versions, API rollout flags and eventual retirement of direct Supabase writes. Existing optional-column fallbacks must not hide missing required finance/custody rules on the new API.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Documentation drift examples:</strong> README dependency versions lag the manifest; September docs claim browse downloads the entire fleet, but current <code>marketplaceService.ts</code> calls filtered guest/authenticated search RPCs with limit/offset; multiple historical “proposed” items now have later migrations. Prefer current call sites and latest applicable SQL definitions, while treating deployment as separately unverified.</p>
''')

section('architecture','3. Recommended Node.js architecture', '''
<p><strong>Recommendation: TypeScript + Fastify modular monolith, PostgreSQL, and a separately runnable worker.</strong> This fits the existing functional-handler proposal and SQL-heavy domain better than an immediate microservice or ORM rewrite. Use Node.js 24 LTS for the initial compatibility baseline, pinning a tested patch and dependency lockfile at implementation. The official release page currently labels Node 24 LTS and Node 26 Current; recheck at kickoff. <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/about/previous-releases">Node release policy</a>.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Recommended implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>HTTP and contracts</td><td>Fastify, repository-owned JSON Schema/Ajv and typed DTOs (for example the compatible TypeBox type-provider pair); @fastify/swagger for generated OpenAPI, Swagger UI restricted to development/staff. Explicit output schemas prevent broad row serialization. <a href="https://fastify.dev/docs/latest/Reference/Validation-and-Serialization/">Fastify schemas</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security</td><td>@fastify/helmet, @fastify/cors with exact web-origin allowlist, @fastify/rate-limit with shared state when replicated; jose for verified JWKS-based tokens where suitable. Select mutually compatible maintained releases. No self-issued identity system or custom OTP cryptography.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data access</td><td>pg with parameterized SQL, one bounded pool per process and explicit transaction runner. Initially call reviewed procedures; use repositories for safe projections. Kysely is an optional later typed-query aid, not a reason to erase SQL constraints. Prisma-style model generation does not capture all procedures/RLS behavior.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Logging/telemetry</td><td>Fastify's Pino logging, redaction and request IDs; OpenTelemetry traces/metrics to a selected backend. Measure authorization rejections, database/pool/lock waits, payment pending age, retries and worker lag. <a href="https://fastify.dev/docs/latest/Reference/Logging/">Fastify logging</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jobs</td><td>Transactional outbox/inbox with idempotent worker. Start with PostgreSQL-backed durable job scheduling or a selected managed queue. Do not add both Redis/BullMQ and a cloud queue without need. Keep existing cron initially or explicitly transfer each job to the worker.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Files/provider adapters</td><td>Authorized object storage adapter, short-lived uploads/downloads, metadata and lifecycle jobs; Razorpay/SMS/routes clients with timeouts, bounded retries and reconciliation. Preserve Supabase Storage initially if doing facade migration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Configuration</td><td>Schema-validate environment at startup; secrets loaded only on server; separate local/test/staging/production, feature flags and versioned DB business policy. Fail startup/readiness on missing critical configuration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tests</td><td>Existing Jest scenarios, Fastify inject HTTP tests, real disposable PostgreSQL integration/concurrency tests and provider sandbox scenarios. Keep the isolated SQL suite as the rule regression base.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<pre>apps/
  customer/                 existing Expo client
  partner/                  existing Expo client
  api/src/
    app.ts                  Fastify composition and routes
    server.ts               process lifecycle
    config/                 validated server configuration
    plugins/                auth, rate limits, logging, errors, db
    modules/
      identity/ catalogue/ fleet/ bookings/ workforce/
      custody/ transport/ payments/ settlements/ support/
      reputation/ notifications/ reporting/
        *.routes.ts         schemas, route guards, HTTP mapping
        *.controller.ts     parse DTO, call service, return projection
        *.service.ts        business commands with explicit dependencies
        *.policy.ts         actor/resource/action checks
        *.repository.ts     parameterized SQL and procedure adapters
        *.schemas.ts        strict request/response contracts
    infrastructure/         identity, storage, payment, routes, event adapters
  worker/src/               outbox, inbox, expiry, reconciliation, cleanup
packages/
  api-contracts/            DTOs, OpenAPI, generated mobile client
  backend-domain/           framework-independent types and rules
  database/                baseline, migrations, SQL, transaction runner
tests/                      HTTP, policy, concurrency, provider contracts</pre>
<p>Register shared packages in <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> deliberately; the existing workspace includes <code>apps/*</code>. Keep backend dependencies and secrets out of Expo bundles. Controllers remain thin; services coordinate transactions and policies; repositories never accept arbitrary SQL/table/RPC names from callers.</p>
<p><strong>Transaction boundary:</strong> authenticate → resolve verified principal to selected account → validate DTO → begin transaction → set trusted transaction-local actor context → authorize resource and lock/version-check → perform state/ledger changes → append audit/outbox → commit → return. Use the same checked-out client for every statement, release in finally, and rollback on failure. <a href="https://node-postgres.com/features/transactions">node-postgres transactions</a>. Do not call remote payment/routing/SMS providers while holding database locks.</p>
<p><strong>Two legitimate migration paths:</strong> (A) retain Supabase Auth and use caller-scoped RPC through an adapter first, preserving RLS; move carefully to direct pg only after reproducing session/claims semantics. Do not proxy every request through the service-role key. (B) replace Supabase with chosen infrastructure: map verified issuer+subject to internal actor/account IDs, port auth.users FKs/auth.uid()/grants and session checks, migrate storage/realtime/cron, and run actor-matrix tests. Existing AWS plans suggest Cognito, PostgreSQL/RDS, object storage and an API/worker deployment, but this assessment does not provision or select that cloud path.</p>
<p><strong>Event delivery:</strong> initially preserve Supabase Realtime behind a client subscription adapter. SSE is a proposed replacement for server-to-client updates; support chat sends continue over REST. Use domain invalidations plus authorized read-back, durable sequence/cursor, reconnect backoff and explicit resync on event retention expiry. Reauthorize on membership changes and token expiry.</p>
''')

section('contracts','4. Proposed REST contract and endpoint catalogue', '''
<p><strong>Every path below is proposed, not an existing Node route.</strong> “Parity” means the underlying workflow exists in reviewed source; it does not mean the HTTP DTO or permission contract is finished. Optional/recommended additions and staff contracts needing external-console review are labeled separately. This is the complete recommended route inventory for the reviewed customer/partner workflows and evidenced staff operations; it is not a completeness claim for repositories outside the supplied folder. A route with a finite action/kind enum covers only the listed commands, never arbitrary SQL/RPC execution.</p>
<p><strong>Refinement delivered:</strong> <a href="openapi-reviewed-subset.json">OpenAPI 3.1.1 draft for 13 operations</a> covers saved addresses (4), public machine search/detail (2), checkout holds (2), and notifications (5). It includes explicit schemas, query serialization, responses/errors, bearer requirements, source file/line anchors, business rules and proposed changes. The other 253 entries remain an inventory for detailed contract work, including identity issuance and staff authorization. This is a deliberately bounded draft, not a complete OpenAPI definition for the whole product. The selected format follows the <a href="https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.1.html">OpenAPI 3.1.1 specification</a>. See <a href="contract-verification.json">static contract checks and unverified items</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Acceptance criteria:</strong> <a href="contract-acceptance-cases.json">28 planned contract cases</a> cover duplicate addresses, partial/null updates, same-phone account isolation, public projection leakage, search bounds and date limits, concurrent holds, expired-hold recovery, notification response shapes and cursor behavior, and session replacement versus authority outage. They are not executed tests; booking-submit cases are explicitly outside the detailed OpenAPI subset pending the full booking DTO. The draft includes illustrative request and response examples, without real credentials or identities.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Corrected contract</th><th>Source behavior and recommended mapping</th><th>Remaining implementation work</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Checkout holds and booking retry</td><td>Hold acquire reuses the live ID and original expiry. Use 200 for acquire-or-reuse because the RPC does not return a created flag. Missing/expired hold at booking submit triggers atomic availability recheck; only a live mismatched hold is rejected.</td><td>Preserve database locking and constraints. Confirm the proposed tighter ordinary-booking window for the hold adapter. Add request-hash mismatch detection separately; existing request-ID replay alone does not provide it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public machine discovery</td><td>Keep default24/cap60, offset0..100000 and the six existing sorts. Public detail uses the safe guest search projection with machine ID; never unrestricted fetchMachine. Same safe fields for logged-in callers.</td><td>Audit authenticated client dependencies before replacing search_machines_filtered. Bounds/date/coordinate pairing and strict query parsing need service checks. The draft retains guest snake_case fields and legacy rupee rate numerics for display; charged amounts still use explicit minor-unit DTOs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Notification responses</td><td>Read-flag RPC boolean maps to 204/404. Bulk-read RPCs return void, so use 204 without a fabricated updatedCount. Target RPC returns state context, not kind/targetId. Inbox default40/cap100; no current category filter.</td><td>Map keys and cursor tuples; reauthorize destination resources. asOf is statement time, not snapshot isolation. Explicit audience admission is an API policy addition to recipient scoping and needs multi-persona confirmation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saved address writes</td><td>Current client normalizes label/address for deduplication; current database has ownership RLS but no normalized uniqueness, length limits or coordinate-pair constraint. Partial PATCH and strict paired coordinates are proposed safeguards.</td><td>Approve limits, clean legacy duplicates, add atomic uniqueness using separate normalized columns, and import device-local local_* IDs through client migration. Omitted fields stay unchanged; clearing coordinates requires both null.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profile and operator identity</td><td>Remove unsupported generic notificationPreferences from profile PATCH. Basic edits are name/businessName/city; account type is not a role switch. Assigned operator read exposes name/assignedAt/rating/reviewCount or null, without an invented operatorCode.</td><td>Keep compliance and payout fields behind dedicated validation and permissions; confirm whether any preference feature is actually required.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Notation:</strong> P = path parameters; Q = query parameters; B = JSON body; H = header. A = authenticated, admitted account with active device session; C = customer; O = owner; Op = operator; B in the authorization column = authorized booking participant, with role-specific field projection; G = guest-safe read; S = staff with both capability and resource scope. Public/OTP routes have rate limits but no bearer requirement. Resource IDs are UUIDs unless explicitly catalogue codes, provider order IDs or operator codes.</p>
<p><strong>Participation is not blanket access:</strong> B is shorthand for an operation-specific policy to be derived from the effective SQL. It never grants every customer, owner or assigned operator the full booking row, finances, evidence or custody codes. Operators already use restricted projections and money-free inbox snapshots. Staff capability names in the broad catalogue are proposed mappings, not verified grants; validate them against the external console before implementation.</p>
<p><strong>Common response/error contract:</strong> successful JSON wraps the stated payload as <code>{data:..., meta:{requestId,...}}</code>; lists use <code>{data:[...],page:{limit,nextCursor},meta:{requestId}}</code> except explicitly retained limit/offset searches. 204 has no body. Error responses are <code>{error:{code,message,details?},requestId}</code>, with sanitized field errors only. All routes may return 400 malformed request, 413 body too large, 415 wrong content type, 422 invalid fields, 429 throttled (Retry-After), 500 unexpected failure or 503 unavailable. Protected routes also return 401 invalid/expired/replaced session, 403 forbidden action, and 404 missing/non-visible resource; avoid existence disclosure. The catalogue adds each route's domain errors. Infrastructure probes use their documented minimal response rather than the JSON envelope.</p>
<p><strong>Pagination:</strong> Page = <code>{limit?:1..100,cursor?:opaque}</code>, default 25 proposed, stable <code>(created_at,id)</code> ordering for history. Cursor binds the filters/sort and is validated. Existing catalogue/reviews expose offset; preserve that during parity, cap depth and add appropriate keyset/search-session semantics later. Do not silently replace the existing “total” with an estimate. GET requests have no body.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended write protocol:</strong> strict allowlisted schemas, unknown fields rejected, Content-Type application/json, Idempotency-Key required on booking/payment/custody side effects and recommended on other retryable creation commands. Store actor+operation+key+canonical request hash+receipt atomically; different payload reusing a key → 409. These HTTP idempotency and version fields are proposed additions unless the source explicitly implements them; current checkout request IDs deduplicate submissions but do not compare a stored request hash. Hold acquisition uses its existing actor/window semantics without a required Idempotency-Key. Use expectedVersion/revision on mutable negotiations; return current version on authorized conflicts. Quote IDs are checked for actor, machine, destination, tariff/version, expiry and amount.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Shared DTO</th><th>Proposed fields and authority</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>MachineInput</td><td>type, make, model, year, capacity, registrationNumber where required, lifetimeWorkingHours, rateDaily/rateHourly/rateMonthly, discounts, serviceRadiusKm, operatorIncluded, operatorAvailableOnRequest, operatorRateBasis/rate where applicable, location {address,city,lat,lng}, featureCodes[], uploadedPhotoIds[], modelId?/variantId?. Exact conditional required fields derive from publication RPC. Exclude ownerId, ratings, KYC/moderation approval and generated refCode.</td></tr>
<tr><td>AttachmentListingInput</td><td>attachmentId, machineId?, title?, serialNo?, conditionNotes?, fileId?, rateDaily/rateHourly/rateMonthly, integer quantity, location, permitted status active/inactive/maintenance/archived. Ownership is server-assigned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>BookingInput</td><td>requestId, holdId?, machineId, startDate/endDate, startMinute/durationMinutes or rateMode+rateUnits, site {address,lat,lng}, withOperator, rentalCompanyId?, companyConsentVersion?, rentalOperatorId?/rentalOperatorIsSelf, transportMode?, mobilizationQuoteId?, paymentPlan?, hoursQuoteId?, isPriority?, attachments [{listingId,quantity}], projectTag?, specialInstructions?, selfOperationTermsVersion?/consent. Insurance selection is excluded while current SQL rejects optional protection. The service derives customer/owner identity, permitted provider/mode, duration, reference, status, prices, taxes, commissions, deposit and frozen policy. Do not accept the broad client BookingRow as a server write DTO.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Booking responses</td><td>id/refCode, lifecycle and version, permitted identities, schedule/site, mode/provider, attachment snapshots, commercial amounts/currency, payment summary, custody/return requirements, serverNow and allowedActions. Customer, owner, operator and staff projections differ.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Money and dates</td><td>Transmit charged amounts as integer minor units plus currency (INR presently); preserve arbitrary-precision SQL decimal calculations and explicit rounding before conversion. Do not silently reinterpret existing rupee numerics as paise. Calendar rental days are YYYY-MM-DD with market timezone; event instants are ISO 8601 UTC. Explicit conversion policy is required for hourly windows and client-supplied local dates.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Custody authorization matrix:</strong> pickup code is held by the owner and verified by assigned operator or eligible machine-only customer; site handover code is held by customer and verified by assigned operator; release code is held by customer and verified by assigned operator after financial clearance; dropoff code is held by owner and verified by assigned operator or eligible returning customer. Owner-self confirmation is its own command, only for the enrolled current self-assignment. Preserve exact latest SQL gates and restricted owner-return fallbacks; read participation is not permission to issue or verify every code.</p>
<p><strong>Overtime action matrix:</strong> customer/owner can request and revise within current gates; assigned operators request/revise through the separate overdue-report command with policy acknowledgement and its time window. Owner confirms/rejects; customer approves/disputes; finalize requires the completed or release-requested work state and current participant policy. Only customer prepares release. Staff finance can reconcile existing cash_pending records; new customer cash reporting remains disabled. Request/revision minutes are 1–10,080 and reasons 3–500 characters in the reviewed overtime procedure. A body action cannot confer a role. Remaining staff enum/field details marked in the catalogue are contract-design tasks, not permission to accept arbitrary fields.</p>
<p><strong>Excluded endpoints:</strong> no generic table/RPC executor, public arbitrary status/payment setters, blanket delete of bookings/payouts/evidence, reward redemption, AI chat/extraction, issuer verification, telephony dispatch, full organization member management, automatic payout/refund executor, or global user administration inferred solely from an admin role. Their functionality is absent or separately gated. Scheduled maintenance functions are worker entry points, not public REST routes.</p>
''')

section('data','5. Data model and security', '''
<p><strong>Retain explicit domain relationships and immutable historical snapshots.</strong> Current data is primarily in public with private operational tables; the schemas below are logical modules, not a claim that the normalized proposal is deployed.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Existing model/relationship</th><th>Migration requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Identity</td><td>auth.users ↔ user_profiles; app_registrations; private customer_phone_accounts/session proofs, mobile_app_accounts, account_devices and device_sessions</td><td>Separate phone principal, selected account and partner role. Verify unique issuer/subject mapping and account proof; phone equality alone is not authorization.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catalogue/fleet</td><td>vehicle_categories → manufacturers/models/variants; attachment_categories → attachments; vehicle_attachment_compatibility and policy; owner → machines and attachment_listings; machine_included_attachments</td><td>Preserve technical fit precedence and retired codes. Reference data differs from physical stock. Machine photos currently include URI fields; normalized photo tables are a proposal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compliance</td><td>user → kyc_documents; machine → machine_documents; rental_verifications and immutable review/verification events</td><td>Private immutable evidence/version references, expiry and review authority; company and nominated operator references attach to bookings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rentals</td><td>customer/owner/machine → bookings → booking_attachments, status events, hour quotes, date changes; private checkout_holds/requests and machine_reservations</td><td>Preserve server price/policy/party snapshots and temporal/exclusion rules. Separate reservation lifetime from booking display state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Workforce</td><td>operator_profiles versus owner-scoped operators roster records, roster_invites, booking_assignments, category rates, duty/availability sessions</td><td>Do not collapse person identity and roster membership; preserve legacy phone joins until explicit identity backfill is verified. Assignments freeze rate/provider terms.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator hires</td><td>customer_operator_vehicles → customer_operator_hires linked to customer and operator; customer_preferred_operators</td><td>Coordinate conflicts across machine bookings and operator-only jobs. No independent operator-hire gateway flow should be assumed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Custody/logistics</td><td>booking_pickups/handover OTPs, booking_condition_reports, booking_positions; transport tariffs/quotes/events/collections, mobilization quotes/ledger</td><td>Code audience, TTL/attempts, one-use transitions, evidence completeness and partial attachment returns. Audit snapshot reports missing new custody objects; reconcile before use.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Finance</td><td>booking → payment_orders, booking_overtime/events, rental_deposit_events, payouts → payout_events/payout_tax_lines; assignment → wage_adjustments/operator_wage_receipts; operator access plans/orders/entitlements/refunds separate</td><td>Track obligations, verified collections and external settlement evidence separately. Enforce unique provider IDs and transitions; no float arithmetic or retrospective reprice.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support/reputation</td><td>support_tickets/conversations/messages/agent_status, disputes, job_incidents; machine_damage_claims and customer_damage_claims; booking_ratings/customer_reviews/rental_role_feedback/replies/reports</td><td>Preserve perspective and time-window authorization. Consolidating tables is optional redesign, not required parity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Engagement</td><td>saved_addresses, notifications, app_feedback, machine_favorites, demand_signals/offers, referral_private attribution</td><td>Private per-account ownership, normalized deduplication and feature-gated consent; no invented reward economy.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Validation:</strong> enforce UUID/code formats, E.164 normalization, bounded text/arrays, paired valid date ranges, positive integer quantities, finite bounded coordinates, nonnegative prices with explicit scale, finite enumerations and cross-field exclusivity. Rating values follow the established scale (booking ratings 1–5); meter values and returned/damaged quantities must not exceed policy/line bounds. Server checks stock, fit, publication, licence windows, document versions, consent versions, role and quote validity at the write, even after a successful preview.</p>
<p><strong>Authorization:</strong> establish a route/action/resource/field matrix and deny by default. Apply policy in service and database. Use restricted non-owner runtime DB roles without BYPASSRLS; transaction-local verified context, safe search_path and restricted EXECUTE for privileged procedures. Table owners usually bypass RLS, so merely enabling it is insufficient. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/ddl-rowsecurity.html">PostgreSQL RLS</a>. Staff finance/dispatch/contact access must retain capability and geography/booking scope, not become a universal admin bypass.</p>
<p><strong>API hardening:</strong> TLS, secure headers, exact CORS origins, trusted-proxy configuration, body/upload/query/time limits, strict server-owned schemas and parameterized SQL. Escape text at presentation; do not use lossy “sanitize everything” rules for names or evidence. Prevent SSRF by accepting server-issued file IDs rather than arbitrary fetch URLs. Use bearer auth in mobile secure storage; if browser cookies are chosen, add Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite and CSRF protection. CORS is not authorization.</p>
<p><strong>Rate-limit recommendation, to tune:</strong> OTP send at most one per phone per 60 seconds plus rolling phone/device/IP budgets; bounded verification attempts per challenge; tighter custody-code, payment-order, upload and support-message limits; cheap per-actor position limits and per-connection event caps. Distributed replicas must share counters. Do not rely on the existing client auth cooldown to stop direct API abuse. Avoid rates that prevent validated provider webhook retries.</p>
<p><strong>Secrets and privacy:</strong> environment-specific managed secrets, workload identity where available, rotation and startup validation; never place database/service-role/Razorpay/webhook/routes secrets in EXPO_PUBLIC variables. Do not log JWTs, phone OTPs, custody codes, phone numbers, KYC contents, bank data, raw evidence, signed URLs or precise movement trails. Public photos can be cached; KYC, condition and identity media require fresh authorization and short-lived URLs. Scan/quarantine uploads and strip unnecessary public-photo metadata; retain original evidentiary material according to approved policy.</p>
<p><strong>Audit:</strong> append actor/account/session, action, subject, timestamp, request/causation IDs, policy/version, result and safe before/after data for bookings, prices, custody, staffing, verification, document/contact access, financial changes, moderation and deletion. Separate security logs from customer-visible business history; restrict log access and retention. Object evidence should not be overwritten; corrections append new records.</p>
<p><strong>Payment integrity:</strong> preserve raw webhook bytes, verify HMAC with constant-time comparison, deduplicate by provider event ID and payment/order uniqueness, check captured amount/currency/obligation, tolerate duplicate and out-of-order delivery, and acknowledge only after durable inbox persistence. Capture may arrive after a failed client return; financial terminal states cannot regress. Reconciliation handles ambiguous provider timeouts. <a href="https://razorpay.com/docs/webhooks/validate-test/">Razorpay webhook guidance</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Versioning:</strong> /v1 plus versioned schemas, additive compatible evolution, deprecation telemetry and a tested mobile compatibility window. Server policy snapshots and consent versions are independent of URL versions. Use expand/contract DB migrations; do not drop fields while older supported apps/admin callers still depend on them.</p>
''')

section('performance','6. Performance and optimization priorities', '''
<p>Impact and effort below are estimates for planning. Existing SQL already includes FK indexes, RLS optimization, overlap protection, filtered search and history pagination; inspect final schema/index definitions and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on safe representative data before adding duplicate indexes.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Recommendation</th><th>Impact</th><th>Effort</th><th>Verification</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>P0</td><td>Preserve database overlap/exclusion rules, row locks and idempotent booking/payment/custody writes</td><td>Very high correctness and contention safety</td><td>High</td><td>Concurrent last-slot, same request key, duplicate capture and return-race tests; no duplicate obligations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P0</td><td>Reconcile schema and use narrow public/role response projections</td><td>Very high correctness/privacy; lower bytes</td><td>Medium</td><td>Required-object checks, unauthorized actor tests, no PII on guest searches or unrelated booking reads.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P0</td><td>Bound every page, bulk ID list, date range and report; keep existing server-side browse</td><td>High latency/memory reduction</td><td>Low–medium</td><td>No unbounded selects; stable pagination tests under inserts; maximum response size monitored.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P0</td><td>Connection budget and transaction discipline</td><td>High availability</td><td>Low–medium</td><td>Budget API replicas × pool + worker pools + rolling deploy overlap + ops headroom below DB capacity. Queue/time-limit pool acquisition; always release; measure pool wait.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Candidate history indexes: bookings(customer_id,created_at,id), bookings(owner_id,created_at,id), booking_assignments(operator_id,state,offered_at,id), payouts(payee_user_id,created_at,id), notifications(recipient_id,audience,created_at,id), support_messages(ticket_id,created_at,id)</td><td>High for growing history</td><td>Medium</td><td>Confirm actual column names/predicates, existing indexes and query plans. Partial unread/open indexes only when predicate/selectivity supports them; no blind index creation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Optimize resource/time availability and geographic search</td><td>High at catalogue growth</td><td>Medium–high</td><td>Reuse reservation range indexes; add appropriate spatial index or bounded geographic prefilter only after validating stored coordinates/query representation. Measure filtered sort before proposing PostGIS migration.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Outbox/inbox plus retry/DLQ and reconciler; move heavy PDFs, media scans and provider followups off request path</td><td>High resilience and latency</td><td>Medium–high</td><td>Worker restart/lost acknowledgement tests, deduplicated effects, observable oldest-job age and failed-job recovery.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Direct object upload, photo resizing and CDN for public listing images</td><td>High bandwidth/API memory savings</td><td>Medium</td><td>Bounded signed upload, verified completion, private-file isolation and orphan cleanup; thumbnails do not alter evidence originals.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Batch related reads and calculate earnings/utilisation aggregates in SQL</td><td>Medium–high</td><td>Medium</td><td>Compare against canonical booking/ledger samples; prevent join multiplication across attachments/payments/assignments. Separate cash received, liability and earned revenue.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1</td><td>Request, SQL, provider and worker telemetry with actionable alerts</td><td>High operational impact</td><td>Medium</td><td>Trace a booking through provider capture and event delivery; alert on payment pending age, code failures, schema errors, job lag, DB saturation and reconciliation mismatch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>ETags and short caches for catalogue, public policy and photo manifests</td><td>Medium latency/load improvement</td><td>Low–medium</td><td>Version-keyed invalidation; authorization-aware private caches. Never treat cached availability/quote/payment status as final write authority.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>Coalesce GPS/events and send resource invalidations instead of whole row broadcasts</td><td>Medium–high for realtime scale</td><td>Medium</td><td>Freshness budget, sequence gaps/reconnect, session revocation and queue backpressure tests.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2</td><td>Compress substantial text/JSON at ingress or @fastify/compress; HTTP keep-alive</td><td>Medium on slow mobile links</td><td>Low</td><td>CPU-versus-bytes benchmarks; skip already compressed media, small payloads, SSE buffering and sensitive reflected responses as appropriate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3</td><td>Read replicas, distributed cache, table partitioning or service extraction</td><td>Conditional</td><td>High</td><td>Adopt only after measurements. Payment/custody read-after-write stays on authoritative primary. Define replication lag tolerance before replica reads.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>node-postgres provides connection pooling; keep it bounded and ensure checked-out clients return on every path. <a href="https://node-postgres.com/features/pooling">Pooling guidance</a>. A database proxy is a later measured option, not a substitute for transaction discipline or capacity budgeting.</p>
''')

section('roadmap','7. Implementation roadmap', '''
<table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Scope and dependencies</th><th>Exit gate</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>0. Decisions and reproducible baseline</td><td>Approve facade versus replacement, current schema versus redesign, identity provider, deployment/queue/storage choices, data preservation and launch scope. Reconcile hosted drift; obtain external-console contracts. Inventory latest effective functions, grants/RLS, triggers, storage and cron; classify obligations and unresolved policies.</td><td>Clean local DB replay or reviewed complete baseline; actor/action matrix; agreed policy/state transitions and capability checks; exact compatibility plan. No destructive migration without confirmed data policy.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1. Foundation MVP</td><td>Fastify/TypeScript scaffolding, strict schemas/OpenAPI, errors/logging, auth/account selection/device session, restricted DB role, profile and saved addresses. Keep current identity initially unless replacement is explicitly selected.</td><td>Real phone/app/account login → persist address → restart/read → cross-account denial; revoked session fails; migration readiness and health checks pass. This is an engineering MVP, not a full rental launch.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Catalogue and supply</td><td>Guest search, owner fleet, publication/evidence, object uploads, attachments/fit/stock, dated availability and blocks. Depend on approved catalogue and compliance rules.</td><td>Verified machine publishable; unapproved machine hidden; public responses sanitized; compatible items with insufficient stock cannot be booked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. End-to-end rental pilot</td><td>Atomic hold/request/recovery, acceptance/staffing, provider payments, operated or machine-only custody, cancellation and return, minimal support/manual finance operations, durable event delivery and expiry jobs. Select supported pilot mode; disable incomplete modes explicitly.</td><td>Successful real-device multi-persona journey and provider sandbox verification; concurrent last-slot and duplicate-event tests; wrong actor cannot read code/advance state; refund liabilities and manual settlement responsibilities visible. Required safety/verification/payment gates cannot be deferred.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Existing feature parity</td><td>All remaining roster/owner-self/operator-only hire flows, transport, negotiated hours, overtime, damage/deposit/wage disputes, access plans, reviews, demand/referrals, full reports and account purge. Bring staff API and external console into contract testing.</td><td>Every parity endpoint maps to service/RPC scenario; unresolved features remain explicitly gated. Cross-app and admin regression suite passes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Production hardening and cutover</td><td>Load/soak tests, restore drill, monitoring/on-call, provider live setup, OTP/placeholder removal, privacy/retention review, backups, rollout flags, client-version compatibility and rollback rehearsal. Transfer each webhook/job owner once.</td><td>Agreed SLO/restore evidence; reconciled finance/custody; monitored staged traffic. No uncoordinated dual writers. Rollback after new writes includes reconciliation/replay, not just changing a URL.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Approved enhancements</td><td>OS push, server-rendered invoice PDFs, automated refunds/payouts, richer spatial search, normalized schema, new markets/currencies, issuer verification or other integrations only with separate requirements and funding.</td><td>Each enhancement has approved business policy, contracts, measurable benefit, security tests and migration/rollback plan.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Dependency spine:</strong> identity/account isolation → fleet verification/publication → availability/compatibility → atomic booking/pricing → staffing/payment → custody/returns → settlement and reporting. Support, observability, event recovery and authorization span every phase. Do not postpone staffing/condition/financial dependencies needed by a selected pilot journey.</p>
<p><strong>Testing priorities:</strong> (1) unauthorized cross-account access including same-phone individual/company accounts; (2) spoofed role/provider/status/money fields; (3) two simultaneous bookings and operator overlaps across hire types; (4) expired hold/quote/licence and stale revision; (5) duplicate and reordered payment callbacks plus crash after provider success; (6) custody-code replay, missing evidence and partial return; (7) cancellation after dispatch with unreturned equipment and unpaid/refund obligations; (8) worker retries, event reconnect and deleted-session revocation; (9) exact old-client response/error compatibility; (10) restore and cutover reconciliation.</p>
<p><strong>Effort planning:</strong> do not estimate this as basic CRUD. The largest work is reconstructing database authority and security, preserving multi-track booking/finance invariants, and migrating provider/session/realtime behavior. Size individual vertical slices after phase 0; no calendar commitment is justified without team capacity and approved scope.</p>
''')

